From d3c0d6f4888bbc0196cb2cebd3897d1106d82cea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yihou <yihou@amd.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 05:55:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] fix(router): decode SGLang's bigram BlockStored tokens in
 the Rust router

The Python half of this fix is already on the branch (6e6fdb7); this is the
other half, which that commit deliberately left out because every run in the
merge experiment used --router-backend python (the default).

With EAGLE/MTP on, SGLang keys its radix tree on bigrams, so each stored page
reports its tokens as the overlapping pairs `(t[i], t[i+1])` instead of bare
ints. `as_u32_vec` read each element as an integer, and a pair is not an
integer, so `filter_map` dropped every one of them: the view stays permanently
empty, `cache_hits` is pinned at 0, and kv-aware routing degrades to load
balancing on exactly the deployments that need it most -- silently, since
nothing in the pipeline errors.

Take the first element of each pair. That rebuilds `t[start:end]`, the same flat
slice `hash_request` chunks on the query side, and radix nodes split on page
boundaries so the two chunkings stay aligned.

Cherry-picked from liyingli's PR #56 (01b0534); the inline unit test comes with
it unchanged. Added `subscriber_decodes_bigram_tokens_under_mtp` to the ZMQ
integration suite as well, because the failure is silent and the in-crate test
does not cover the real socket + msgpack decode path where it would be met.

Verified by control rather than by argument, in the built image on chi2879:
reverting `as_u32_any` and rerunning the integration test fails with
`left: 0, right: 2` -- the bug verbatim, every pair dropped -- and restoring it
passes. Whole crate: 70 tests, 0 failures.

A live A/B of the two router binaries against the running PD pair was tried
first and rejected as non-discriminating: that prefill leg runs without
--speculative-algorithm, so its wire carries plain ints and the *unpatched*
binary also read 51 hits.

Co-authored-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
